[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first. Each problem now has an issue of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Fixing the parser leaves the booking-v3 form hint and the one-day moves open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the
  day first, so day and month swap. Any channel, any form.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it
  says, and the form reads it day first. Only the `form` channel of booking-v3 tenants. The parser
  fix does not touch it.
- **`one-day move`**: the booking lands a day early, or on different days in different time zones.
  Nothing is swapped. These are the reports the desk already noted as one-day moves
  (`desk-notes.json`); the #4471 page still counts them under `parser`.

## How the reports were placed

- The export pages overlap: some reports come twice. One id, one report.
- A report's cause comes from its tenant (locale and booking form, `locales.ts`), not from intake's
  tag. Intake guessed from the reporter's browser, and its `hint` tags are wrong both ways: the
  `tagged as` column shows it.
- Subjects say nothing about cause ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on
  them). All three problems share subjects such as "Booking on the wrong date".

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A count is not a cause. Before marking a big ticket known, read its reports against where they
  came from.
- Merging on subject puts different bugs in one thread. Split them before the fix ships.

